 "chunks": [
  {
   "text": "In a parallel world, the analogous hypothesis has been proved for decades.",
   "start": 0.75,
   "end": 5.45
  },
  {
   "text": "Do arithmetic on a clock with p hours, p prime,",
   "start": 5.45,
   "end": 9.05
  },
  {
   "text": "and count the solutions of y² + y = x³ − x.",
   "start": 9.05,
   "end": 13.775
  },
  {
   "text": "For p equal to thirteen, there are fifteen, plus one extra point at infinity.",
   "start": 13.775,
   "end": 19.225
  },
  {
   "text": "Each x gives two, one or no values of y, one on average,",
   "start": 20.175,
   "end": 23.925
  },
  {
   "text": "so you'd expect about p plus one.",
   "start": 23.925,
   "end": 26.075
  },
  {
   "text": "The surprise is how close it stays: the error is never more than two root p,",
   "start": 26.075,
   "end": 30.4
  },
  {
   "text": "for every prime.",
   "start": 30.4,
   "end": 31.788
  },
  {
   "text": "Hasse proved this for curves like this one in the 1930s,",
   "start": 31.788,
   "end": 35.188
  },
  {
   "text": "Weil the general version for every curve in the 1940s,",
   "start": 35.188,
   "end": 38.025
  },
  {
   "text": "and Deligne went much further in 1974.",
   "start": 38.025,
   "end": 41.188
  },
  {
   "text": "Each such curve has a zeta function, and for this one,",
   "start": 41.188,
   "end": 44.225
  },
  {
   "text": "the bound is exactly its Riemann Hypothesis.",
   "start": 44.225,
   "end": 47.2
  }
 ],
